WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.220 --> 00:00:02.595
COLT MCANLIS: In this episode
of Cloud Performance Atlas,

00:00:02.595 --> 00:00:04.850
I take a look at the right
way to profile a CDN.

00:00:04.850 --> 00:00:06.502
Will our latency hit the mark?

00:00:06.502 --> 00:00:07.460
Stay tuned to find out.

00:00:07.460 --> 00:00:09.387
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:00:10.730 --> 00:00:13.700
In a previous episode, we talked
about how the Google Cloud Load

00:00:13.700 --> 00:00:16.190
Balancer can be
supercharged to leverage CDN

00:00:16.190 --> 00:00:18.560
capabilities for faster
content distribution

00:00:18.560 --> 00:00:20.930
to your users, which
of course kicked off

00:00:20.930 --> 00:00:22.520
a discussion with
multiple developers

00:00:22.520 --> 00:00:24.710
about the nuances
of CDN performance

00:00:24.710 --> 00:00:26.720
and how Google's stacked up.

00:00:26.720 --> 00:00:28.940
Now the minimum
functionality for a CDN

00:00:28.940 --> 00:00:32.180
is caching a requested
asset closer to users

00:00:32.180 --> 00:00:35.540
so that future requests have
the lowest possible latency.

00:00:35.540 --> 00:00:37.580
To see what this looks
like performance-wise,

00:00:37.580 --> 00:00:39.440
we simply set up
an asset on the CDN

00:00:39.440 --> 00:00:43.130
and fetched it via a cURL
command about 500 times or so.

00:00:43.130 --> 00:00:45.800
You know things are working
properly when your graph looks

00:00:45.800 --> 00:00:46.670
something like this.

00:00:46.670 --> 00:00:48.162
You see a bunch
of slower requests

00:00:48.162 --> 00:00:50.120
at the front of your
timeline, and then latency

00:00:50.120 --> 00:00:53.570
drops significantly
once the caching occurs.

00:00:53.570 --> 00:00:56.150
If you run the same test
but from various instances

00:00:56.150 --> 00:00:59.340
distributed globally, you'll get
something that looks like this.

00:00:59.340 --> 00:01:01.430
We placed an asset
on a server in Texas,

00:01:01.430 --> 00:01:03.620
and fetched it from various
points around the world.

00:01:03.620 --> 00:01:06.110
You can see how Google's
Edge Network properly cached

00:01:06.110 --> 00:01:09.520
the asset and moved it closer
to the user for later fetches.

00:01:09.520 --> 00:01:12.330
Now-- oh, by the way, if
you're super interested,

00:01:12.330 --> 00:01:14.210
you can modify your
cURL command to spit out

00:01:14.210 --> 00:01:16.160
all the subsections
of those requests

00:01:16.160 --> 00:01:17.375
and graph those individually.

00:01:17.375 --> 00:01:20.180
Now things like DNS
resolve time, connection,

00:01:20.180 --> 00:01:23.720
and pre-transfer speeds can
vary between CDN providers.

00:01:23.720 --> 00:01:26.480
So it's sometimes nice to see
how these things break out

00:01:26.480 --> 00:01:28.710
so that you can
compare them properly.

00:01:28.710 --> 00:01:32.599
Now, sadly, these tests show a
rough estimate of performance.

00:01:32.599 --> 00:01:34.640
But to really show off
the power of Google's CDN,

00:01:34.640 --> 00:01:36.410
you need to write a bigger test.

00:01:36.410 --> 00:01:38.510
For example, the Cloud
Team built a test

00:01:38.510 --> 00:01:41.570
using Vegeta running on
Google Container Engine, which

00:01:41.570 --> 00:01:45.470
allowed each pod to scale
up to about 50,000 RPS.

00:01:45.470 --> 00:01:48.320
And an even bigger test has
been done by the Cedexis Radar

00:01:48.320 --> 00:01:50.360
Community, which
collects over 14

00:01:50.360 --> 00:01:52.760
billion real-user
measurements each day

00:01:52.760 --> 00:01:56.256
from users all around the
world across providers, which

00:01:56.256 --> 00:01:58.130
means that you can
compare them and see which

00:01:58.130 --> 00:02:00.097
one works for your situation.

00:02:00.097 --> 00:02:02.180
If you're interested in
more information on either

00:02:02.180 --> 00:02:04.856
of those tests, check out
the article for this video,

00:02:04.856 --> 00:02:06.980
linked in the description
below, where you can also

00:02:06.980 --> 00:02:09.770
find more great ideas on
how to optimize your Cloud

00:02:09.770 --> 00:02:12.180
Performance, because when
it comes to performance,

00:02:12.180 --> 00:02:13.370
every millisecond counts.

00:02:13.370 --> 00:02:16.420
[MUSIC PLAYING]

